#: Game.Levels.GCD.L06_cancel_coprime
msgid "Here is the central theorem of this world — and one of the most useful facts in all of number theory.\n"
"\n"
"If §0, and §1 is coprime to §2 (§3), can we conclude §4? In other words: can we *cancel* the common factor §5 from both sides of a congruence?\n"
"\n"
"**Yes** — but only because §6 and §7 are coprime! Without that assumption this would be false: take §8, §9. Then §10 (since §11), yet §12 is false (§13 is not a multiple of §14). Coprimality is essential.\n"
"\n"
"**The key idea:** since §15 and §16 are coprime, we have §17 for some §18 (that's exactly what you proved in Level 4!). Multiplying our congruence's underlying equation by §19 makes the extra factor §20 effectively disappear, leaving exactly what we want.\n"
"\n"
"This proof is long, but every single step is either a §21 (a pure algebra identity, closed by §22) or an §23 that plugs in a hypothesis. Take it one line at a time."
msgstr ""

